.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-NETWORKS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-networks
.Nd define kldload's libvirt networks, and copy machines' addresses into the state database
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm apply
.Nm
.Cm sync
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
does two jobs for the system libvirt,
.Ql qemu:///system .
.Pp
.Cm apply
defines the networks shipped in
.Pa /usr/local/share/kldload-networks/ ,
marks them to start at boot, and starts them:
.Bl -column "kld-zfslab" "192.168.150.0/24" -offset indent
.It Li kld-klab Ta 192.168.150.0/24 Ta klab goldens and sites
.It Li kld-zfslab Ta 192.168.151.0/24 Ta the ZFS test lab
.It Li kld-vms Ta 192.168.152.0/24 Ta other machines
.El
.Pp
libvirt's own
.Li default
network, 192.168.122.0/24, where the Kubernetes cluster lives, is left
alone.
A network that exists is left as it is, and defining one moves no machine
onto it.
.Pp
.Cm sync
teaches the state database, which
.Xr kldload-inventory 8
turns into Ansible's inventory, what libvirt knows about addresses.
Machines are registered without one, since they have not asked for a DHCP
lease yet; an address copied here is what makes a machine an Ansible
target.
For each machine libvirt has:
.Bl -bullet
.It
running, with a lease on any of libvirt's networks \(em matched to the
machine by its MAC address, the newest lease winning \(em or an address
its guest agent reports: that address is recorded;
.It
not running: its address is cleared, so a play cannot reach, through an
address DHCP has since handed on, the wrong machine.
.El
Cluster nodes whose machine is not running are marked offline, and back
ready when it is; a machine the database lists and libvirt no longer has
loses its address.
.Pp
It also rewrites
.Pa /root/.config/wgx/hosts ,
the host list
.Xr wgx 1
checks WireGuard peers against, from the endpoints of the peers this host
has now.
.Pp
The timer
.Pa kldload-inventory-sync.timer
runs
.Cm sync
every minute.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm apply , up
The default.
Define, autostart and start each shipped network, report the counts, and
list the networks.
Needs root.
.It Cm sync
As above.
Needs root.
.It Cm list , ls
Every network of the system libvirt, its state, whether it starts at boot,
its address and prefix, and its purpose.
.It Cm help , Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_NETWORK_DIR
Read the network definitions from this directory instead.
.It Ev LIBVIRT_DEFAULT_URI
The libvirt to use; default
.Ql qemu:///system .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/local/share/kldload-networks/*.xml
The network definitions, in libvirt's format.
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/networks.log
What each run did, per machine.
.It Pa /root/.config/wgx/hosts
The derived
.Xr wgx 1
host list.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
libvirt is absent or did not answer within 30 seconds, a network failed
to define or start, or
.Cm apply
or
.Cm sync
was run without root.
.It 2
An unknown command.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on 2026-09-29, on a kldload desktop whose per-class networks had
never been defined (see
.Sx CAVEATS )
and on a freshly installed hypervisor.
.Ss The networks (list)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kldload-networks list
  NETWORK        STATE    AUTOSTART    SUBNET             PURPOSE
  ────────────── ──────── ──────────── ────────────────── ───────────────
  default        yes      yes          192.168.122.1/24   kubernetes cluster
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Defining them (apply)
On the hypervisor, the first time, and again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-networks apply
Networks: 3 defined, 3 started, 0 already present, 0 failed.

  NETWORK        STATE    AUTOSTART    SUBNET             PURPOSE
  ────────────── ──────── ──────────── ────────────────── ───────────────
  default        yes      yes          192.168.122.1/24   kubernetes cluster
  kld-klab       yes      yes          192.168.150.1/24   klab goldens + sites
  kld-vms        yes      yes          192.168.152.1/24   operator VMs
  kld-zfslab     yes      yes          192.168.151.1/24   ZFS test lab
# (exited 0)
# kldload-networks apply
Networks: 0 defined, 0 started, 3 already present, 0 failed.
\&...
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
A definition that is not valid XML
.Pq Ev KLDLOAD_NETWORK_DIR :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# KLDLOAD_NETWORK_DIR=/var/tmp/badnets kldload-networks apply
  ! failed to define kld-broken — see /var/log/kldload/networks.log
Networks: 0 defined, 0 started, 0 already present, 1 failed.
\&...
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss Addresses into the database (sync)
A throwaway clone of a golden was started, synced, powered off, and synced
again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-networks sync
Leases seen: 9 (0 stale).  VMs with an address: 0 → 1.
\&...
# kldload-networks sync
Leases seen: 9 (1 stale).  VMs with an address: 1 → 0.
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
With nothing to change:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-networks sync
Leases seen: 8 (0 stale).  VMs with an address: 0 → 0.
Only VMs libvirt reports as running are given an address.

  NOTE: no VM address changed. Either every VM already had its address,
  or the lease hostnames do not match any registered VM name.
  Compare:  virsh net-dhcp-leases <net>   against   kldload-db dump
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-networks sync
kldload-networks: sync must run as root (list does not)
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "  ! failed to define NAME \(em see /var/log/kldload/networks.log"
.It "  ! failed to start NAME \(em see /var/log/kldload/networks.log"
libvirt's reason is in the log.
The other networks are still applied; the exit status is 1.
.It "kldload-networks: libvirtd did not answer after 30s"
.It "kldload-networks: VERB must run as root (list does not)"
.It "NOTE: no VM address changed. ..."
From
.Cm sync ,
when leases were seen and no address moved: either nothing needed to, or
no lease belongs to a registered machine.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr wgx 1 ,
.Xr kldload-db 8 ,
.Xr kldload-estate 8 ,
.Xr kldload-inventory 8 ,
.Xr kldload-netplan 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Cm sync
was written after 2026-08-17, when ten registered machines had no address
and the inventory had no machine in it: the call that records one had no
caller.
Its first version reported
.Dq 11 VMs updated
over an unchanged database, and it counts addresses before and after
since.
On 2026-08-30 two machines on one desktop claimed one address, a stopped
machine's and the one DHCP had given it since; stopped machines lose
theirs since.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: its
.Dq is this network running
test failed every time, a pipeline killed by its own early exit, so
running
.Cm apply
again tried to start every network and reported success;
run as a user it asked the user's own libvirt, and
.Cm sync
died writing its log;
and every stopped machine was counted as a stale address on every run,
every minute.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
The three networks are defined only where
.Cm apply
runs.
kldload's first boot runs it on a PRO hypervisor; a FREE install ships the
definitions and does not define them, and no kldload tool yet puts a
machine on them by name.
Run
.Cm apply
by hand to have them.
.Pp
Their ranges are also in the network plan,
.Xr kldload-netplan 8 ,
which this tool does not read yet.
